{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Axjg1iTtXnhPwTozTHwJV","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"No, a new thread-type identifying parameter (e.g., ThreadType) was **not** added to the error handler context; the final decision was to use a single handler implementation, relying on the existing `ErrorHandlerContext` fields (`topic()`, `processorNodeId()`, `taskId()`) to distinguish global-thread from regular stream-thread errors.\\\\n\\\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\\\n- On 2026-01-27T06:12:42.000000Z, Matthias J. Sax stated that no multiple handlers or ThreadType parameter are needed, because the handler is invoked with an `ErrorHandlerContext` that already provides enough information to distinguish the case (e.g., `topic()`, `processorNodeId()`, and `taskId()`) [mail:0cd1da9b3ea5d6d07a9cf457].\\\\n- Arpit Goyal agreed, saying \\\\\\\"Client can use the single handler implementation to support error handling for both Stream Thread and Global thread. There is no need to introduce ThreadType parameter or another configuration for the same.\\\\\\\" [mail:c8e42cc6bc8a5e4ae0fb0d26][mail:7e2de452e42af04db5c25b47][mail:e95cf0a05fd47c30fbb99cfa][mail:78462d1ca384f259d58e37cc]\\\\n- Lucas Brutschy confirmed this answered his query about distinguishing globalThread vs StreamsThread errors [mail:db48109864286cad76518f6e][mail:bd0baeb6576bc00102b33e78].\\\\n\\\\nWhat could not be determined: Nothing; the final decision is clearly documented in the cited records.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:0cd1da9b3ea5d6d07a9cf457\\\",\\\"mail:c8e42cc6bc8a5e4ae0fb0d26\\\",\\\"mail:7e2de452e42af04db5c25b47\\\",\\\"mail:e95cf0a05fd47c30fbb99cfa\\\",\\\"mail:78462d1ca384f259d58e37cc\\\",\\\"mail:db48109864286cad76518f6e\\\",\\\"mail:bd0baeb6576bc00102b33e78\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":95.299,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":13,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":null,\\\"c